{"agent":"architecture","findings":[{"id":"arch-001","file":"src/roonseek/transfer.py","line":139,"title":"SlskdTransferClient's documented POST/GET-only boundary is now false because it can also DELETE","category":"abstraction","cwe":null,"severity":"low","agent_confidence":70,"in_diff":false,"intent_doc_match":null,"problem":"The class docstring describes the client's safety boundary as: 'Exposes only the download enqueue (POST) and status fetch (GET) endpoints on the /api/v0/transfers/downloads surface.' The diff adds a third endpoint, cancel_download (DELETE), which is a destructive operation, but leaves that docstring unchanged. The docstring is how this codebase states the dry-run and least-capability boundary between this client and SlskdHttpClient, so the documented scope no longer matches the code.","current_code":"    Exposes only the download enqueue (POST) and status fetch (GET) endpoints on\n    the /api/v0/transfers/downloads surface.","fix_hint":"Update the class docstring to list cancel (DELETE) as part of the transfer-only surface, and add the T-02-02 encoding note to cancel_download's docstring as the sibling methods have.","why_it_matters":"Later reviewers and agents use this docstring to judge which side effects the transfer client is allowed to have. A stale boundary statement hides that the client can now destroy transfers.","silenced_marker_nearby":false},{"id":"arch-002","file":"src/roonseek/transfer.py","line":67,"title":"A failed cancel raises TransferEnqueueError, an error type documented for enqueue and status fetch only","category":"abstraction","cwe":null,"severity":"low","agent_confidence":55,"in_diff":false,"intent_doc_match":null,"problem":"cancel_download goes through the shared _request_json, so any non-2xx, 429 or 5xx response from the DELETE raises TransferEnqueueError. That type's docstring says it is 'Raised on HTTP errors from slskd during download enqueue or status fetch.' Callers that branch on the exception type cannot tell